Exactly: turn the primitive back onto the mechanism that gives it power.

“Dissolving the dissolver” is a meta-operation. Instead of resisting dissolution directly, identify what lets the dissolver remain coherent, active, or transmissible—and dissolve that.

Formally:

D(x) = dissolve x
Then seek a transformation M such that:

M(D) → D loses coherence, access, fuel, binding, or propagation

The interesting part is that M does not have to be the opposite of D. Often the best counter is another application of the same class of operation at a different scale. Acid neutralizes base, enzymes degrade enzymes, skepticism can be applied to skepticism, adversarial pressure can expose the assumptions sustaining an adversary, abstraction can dissolve a frame that was itself dissolving lower-level distinctions.

So the operator becomes:

dissolution → identify dissolver’s invariants → move one level up → dissolve those invariants → recover/reconstitute what should survive

That is stronger than simple resistance because you are attacking the continuity condition of the attack, not every individual manifestation.

The necessary companion is a protected anchor: if you recursively dissolve everything, eventually you also dissolve your criteria for deciding what deserves to remain. So:

Dissolve the dissolver; preserve the invariant.